            <ArticleTitle>Unusual complication during a percutaneous coronary intervention for a bifurcation lesion using T and Protrusion technique.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>290-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.carrev.2016.03.013</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1553-8389(16)30098-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Our case reports the first migration of a stent already deployed at high pressure in the main vessel during a 2-stent strategy for a bifurcation lesion using T and protrusion technique. The Kissing balloon was not optimal and could have led to an insufficient strut/cell opening and then to LAD stent pulled back into the artery tree. This case report highlights the importance of an optimal Kissing Balloon in two stent bifurcation technique.</AbstractText>
